{"type":"system","subtype":"background_tasks_changed","tasks":[{"task_id":"b510trmkc","task_type":"local_bash","description":"D=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_4_repair_1\npython3 - <<EOF\ns=open(\"/tmp/t/v1.py\").read()\nold_doc=s[:s.index(\"Description of the refined model follows.\")]\nnew_doc='''\"\"\"Mildly rigged, symmetric trick coins in the streak-weary pseudo-flip judgment.\n\nRefinement of `streak_weary_chance_pseudoflip` (described below). Single change:\nwhat people imagine a trick coin to be. The incumbent's trick coin has a\nheads-skewed Beta(2 s, 2 (1 - s)) prior over its heads rate (uniform spread,\nfitted heads lean s, whose posterior sits at ~0.49, i.e. no lean). Here the\ntrick coin is H/T-symmetric, Beta(kappa / 2, kappa / 2), with its concentration\nkappa fitted: people imagine only mildly rigged coins (large kappa), so a modest\nheads/tails imbalance is weak evidence against chance and balance counts for\nless against switching than with the incumbent's uniform spread. Same number of\nparameters (s replaced by kappa; kappa's prior keeps its upper tail\nconstrained). Addresses the critique that in length-8 pairs where the\nmore-switching sequence is the more lopsided one, people choose the\nmore-switching sequence more often than the incumbent predicts.\n\n'''\ns=new_doc+s[len(old_doc):]\nold='''    # Trick coin with a lopsided prior over its heads rate: Beta(2 s, 2 (1 - s)),\n    # s > 0.5 means coins rigged towards heads are suspected more.\n    # Prior tightened (sigma 0.5): with sigma 1 a degenerate mode at s ~ 0.02\n    # (a near-certain tails-rigged coin) trapped a chain; the data put s near 0.5.\n    s_heads = 0.5\n    # Fitted spread of suspected trick coins: concentration kappa (2 = uniform).\n    log_kappa = pm.Normal(\"log_kappa\", mu=math.log(2.0), sigma=0.75)\n    kappa = pm.Deterministic(\"kappa\", pt.exp(log_kappa))\n    a_h = kappa * s_heads\n    a_t = kappa - a_h'''\nassert old in s\ns=s.replace(old,'''    # Symmetric trick coin, Beta(kappa / 2, kappa / 2) over its heads rate, with\n    # the spread fitted (kappa = 2: uniform, the incumbent's spread; large kappa:\n    # only mildly rigged coins are imagined).\n    log_kappa = pm.Normal(\"log_kappa\", mu=1.5, sigma=0.75)\n    kappa = pm.Deterministic(\"kappa\", pt.exp(log_kappa))\n    a_h = 0.5 * kappa\n    a_t = 0.5 * kappa''')\nopen(\"$D/candidate.py\",\"w\").write(s)\nEOF\ncat > $D/hypothesis.md <<'EOF'\nRefinement of the incumbent `streak_weary_chance_pseudoflip` (people judge a sequence random to the extent their own second-order, streak-weary picture of a fair coin explains it better than the regular generators they suspect — a switch-biased coin, a trick coin and a repeating motif with slips — with pseudo-flip-diluted per-flip evidence, heavy-tailed signed person sensitivity and a side habit). The single change is to the trick coin people imagine: not one leaning towards heads with any degree of rigging equally likely, but a symmetric coin that is only mildly rigged, with how mildly fitted. A modest heads/tails imbalance is then weak evidence against chance, so people weigh balance less against switching. This addresses the critique that in length-8 pairs where the more-switching sequence is also the more lopsided one, people choose the more-switching sequence more often than the incumbent predicts.\nEOF\necho mild_symmetric_trick_coin_streak_weary > $D/model_name.txt\ngrep -n \"s_heads\\|logit_s\" $D/candidate.py\n/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate --candidate-dir $D --responses /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/responses.csv 2>&1 | tail -3"}],"uuid":"49c0ed20-16e2-4269-bf23-74b3802dcdc2","session_id":"adf3e9fa-9a90-4046-a6bb-791723d5569b"}
